[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21375", :title "Memorilla: Latent Semantic Memory for LLMs", :link "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21375", :published-at #inst "2026-11-06T18:03:33.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "Memorilla: Latent Semantic Memory for LLMs", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://dev.to/jlmartel/java-when-life-gives-you-problems-build-a-problemfactory-534a", :title "Java: When Life Gives You Problems, Build a ProblemFactory", :link "https://dev.to/jlmartel/java-when-life-gives-you-problems-build-a-problemfactory-534a", :published-at #inst "2026-11-06T07:00:00.000-00:00", :content "We've already explored PHP's questionable decisions, JavaScript's complicated relationship with logic, Python's ability to produce confidently incorrect results, and C#'s enterprise-grade surprises. Now it's Java's turn. Java is different. Java is serious. Java is strongly typed, object-oriented, carefully specified, and famously obsessed with backward compatibility. This is a language that makes you declare what things are before you're allowed to do anything with them. Surely, then, Java should be relatively free of surprises? Well. Let's talk about that. 1. String Concatenation: Apparently, + Needed a Performance Review Let's start with something that every Java developer has written thousands of times. String message = \"Hello, \" + name + \"!\";\n Perfectly reasonable. Now consider: System.out.println(\"Total: \" + 1 + 2);\n Output: Total: 12\n But: System.out.println(1 + 2 + \" total\");\n Output: 3 total\n Same numbers. Same operator. Different arithmetic. Because Java evaluates these expressions from left to right, and once a string enters the expression, + becomes string concatenation. Fine. We can live with that. But Java has another surprise waiting. Meet StringBuilder Java strings are immutable. Once created, a String cannot be modified. So what happens when you repeatedly append characters? String result = \"\";\n\nfor (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\n    result += i;\n}\n Conceptually, each iteration creates a new string containing the accumulated result plus the new value. As the string grows, increasingly large amounts of data may need to be deal with by the jvm. That's potentially a lot of unnecessary work. Java developers have traditionally solved this problem with StringBuilder. StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();\n\nfor (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\n    result.append(i);\n}\n\nString finalResult = result.toString();\n One mutable buffer, rather than repeatedly rebuilding an immutable string. Much better for this kind of workload. For years, the conventional advice was simple: Use + for simple concatenation. Use StringBuilder when you're building strings in loops. But Java being Java, it couldn't leave things quite that straightforward. Plot Twist: The Compiler Was Already Helping Before Java 9, the Java compiler typically translated ordinary string concatenation expressions into StringBuilder operations behind the scenes. So this: String message = \"Hello, \" + name + \"!\";\n Wasn't necessarily doing the naive string-building work you might imagine. Then Java 9 introduced a new concatenation mechanism using invokedynamic. This allowed the runtime to select and optimize string-concatenation strategies more flexibly, without requiring the compiler to generate an explicit StringBuilder sequence. So now we've got: Strings that can't be changed. A mutable class specifically for building strings. A compiler that historically generated that class automatically. A newer mechanism that can optimize concatenation differently. Performance characteristics that depend on the expression, loop structure, compiler, and runtime. Congratulations. We've turned joining two strings into a discussion about bytecode generation. To be fair, modern Java handles ordinary + concatenation quite well. The real performance concern remains repeated accumulation into a growing string, particularly in loops. An explicit StringBuilder is still a sensible choice there. The lesson: Java's simple syntax can hide considerable compiler and runtime sophistication. And performance advice from 2005 doesn't always survive contact with modern JVMs. 2. Two Integers Are Equal. Until They Aren't. Here's one of Java's most entertaining party tricks. Integer a = 127;\nInteger b = 127;\n\nSystem.out.println(a == b);\n Output: true\n Okay. Now: Integer a = 128;\nInteger b = 128;\n\nSystem.out.println(a == b);\n On a typical JVM configuration: false\n Wait. What? Two integers containing the same number can compare equal or unequal depending on the number. The trick is that Integer isn't the same thing as int. int is a primitive value. Integer is an object wrapper around an integer value. And for objects, == checks reference identity, not numerical equality. But Java caches certain Integer objects. Specifically, Integer.valueOf() is guaranteed to cache values from -128 through 127. Autoboxing uses this mechanism. So when you write: Integer a = 127;\nInteger b = 127;\n Both variables normally refer to the same cached object. But outside the guaranteed range, Java is allowed to create separate objects. Hence the surprising result for 128. There's one more complication: implementations are permitted to cache additional values, so 128 isn't guaranteed to produce false everywhere. Which makes relying on this behavior an even worse idea. The correct comparison: System.out.println(a.equals(b));\n Or compare the underlying primitive values when appropriate. The lesson: In Java, == can mean numerical equality or object identity depending on the types involved. And integer caching makes that distinction particularly easy to overlook. 3. Arrays.asList() Gives You a List. Terms and Conditions Apply. Here's a perfectly ordinary bit of Java: String[] names = {\"Alice\", \"Bob\", \"Charlie\"};\n\nList<String> list = Arrays.asList(names);\n You now have a list. And lists are mutable collections, right? Let's add someone. list.add(\"Dave\");\n Result: java.lang.UnsupportedOperationException\n Excuse me? We created a list. We tried to add an item. And Java objected. Here's the explanation. Arrays.asList() returns a fixed-size list backed by the original array. It isn't a regular, independently resizable ArrayList. You can replace existing elements: list.set(0, \"Zoe\");\n That works. But you can't change the list's size. No adding. No removing. And here's the really interesting part: System.out.println(names[0]);\n Output: Zoe\n Changing the list changed the original array! Because they're backed by the same storage. The reverse is true too. Change an element in the array, and the list reflects that change. So Arrays.asList() gives you something that: Looks like a normal list. Lets you replace elements. Refuses to grow or shrink. Shares its underlying data with the original array. Java also offers List.of(), which creates an unmodifiable list. And new ArrayList<>(...), which creates a separate, resizable list. Three ways to obtain something called a list, with three different sets of rules. The fix when you want an independent, resizable list: List<String> list =\n    new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(names));\n The lesson: In Java, knowing that something implements List doesn't tell you everything about what operations it supports. Sometimes the interface makes a promise that the implementation politely declines to fulfill. 4. Removing an Integer Can Remove the Wrong Integer This one is genuinely nasty. Suppose you have a list of numbers: List<Integer> numbers =\n    new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 20, 55));\n Now you want to remove the number 1. numbers.remove(1);\n What happens? The list becomes: [1, 55]\n You can kind of guess this one. Our friend List has two overloaded remove() methods. One removes an element by index. The other removes an element by value. Conceptually: remove(int index)\nremove(Object value)\n And when you pass the literal integer 1, Java selects the overload accepting a primitive int. So: numbers.remove(1);\n Means: \"Remove the element at index 1.\" Not: \"Remove the integer whose value is 1.\" If you actually want to remove the value 1: numbers.remove(Integer.valueOf(1));\n Now Java selects the object-based overload. Same method name. Same apparent kind of argument. Completely different operation. And because both operations are perfectly valid, the compiler has absolutely nothing to complain about. The lesson: Method overloading, autoboxing, and collection APIs can combine into a surprisingly effective footgun. Sometimes Java's type system is so helpful it confidently does the wrong thing you explicitly asked it to do. 5. Two Decimal Numbers Can Be Equal but Not Equal For our final example, let's involve money. Java provides BigDecimal for decimal arithmetic where precision matters. Unlike ordinary binary floating-point numbers, BigDecimal can represent decimal values exactly. Very useful for financial applications. Now consider: BigDecimal a = new BigDecimal(\"2.0\");\nBigDecimal b = new BigDecimal(\"2.00\");\n\nSystem.out.println(a.equals(b));\n Output: false\n Seriously? Two dollars is not equal to two dollars? Now try: System.out.println(a.compareTo(b) == 0);\n Output: true\n So the numbers compare as equal. But equals() says they're different. Why? Because BigDecimal stores both an unscaled numerical value and a scale. 2.0 and 2.00 represent the same numerical value, but they have different scales. And BigDecimal.equals() considers scale part of equality. compareTo() compares their numerical values instead. That's actually defensible. Scale can matter for certain decimal calculations. But it creates a wonderfully awkward consequence. Consider: Set<BigDecimal> hashSet = new HashSet<>();\n\nhashSet.add(new BigDecimal(\"2.0\"));\nhashSet.add(new BigDecimal(\"2.00\"));\n\nSystem.out.println(hashSet.size());\n Output: 2\n Now: Set<BigDecimal> treeSet = new TreeSet<>();\n\ntreeSet.add(new BigDecimal(\"2.0\"));\ntreeSet.add(new BigDecimal(\"2.00\"));\n\nSystem.out.println(treeSet.size());\n Output: 1\n Two sets. Same values. Different answers about how many distinct elements exist. Why? HashSet uses equals() and hashCode() to determine uniqueness. TreeSet uses ordering comparisons, which for BigDecimal normally means compareTo(). And BigDecimal explicitly defines those operations differently. So Java gives you two completely legitimate definitions of uniqueness for the same numerical values. Imagine discovering that while reconciling financial records. The lesson: Equality, numerical comparison, and collection uniqueness aren't necessarily interchangeable. With BigDecimal, choosing the wrong one can produce very real business problems. Java Is Predictable. You Just Need to Predict the Right Things. Here's what makes Java interesting. Unlike JavaScript, Java generally doesn't let values wander casually between unrelated types. Unlike PHP, it doesn't enthusiastically reinterpret everything based on context. And unlike Python, it tends to insist that you explain exactly what you're trying to do. Java is explicit. Java is disciplined. Java is designed to make large programs easier to reason about. And yet... An integer can be identical to another integer until you increase it by one. A list can refuse to let you add anything. Removing a number can remove a completely different number. Two decimal values can be equal according to one method and unequal according to another. And joining strings can involve compiler transformations, runtime call sites, and an optimization discussion that lasts longer than the code itself. None of this is random. There are carefully documented reasons for every behavior. But that doesn't make the surprises any less entertaining. In fact, it makes them more interesting. Java is a language where even the weirdness comes with a specification. But Wait. Which Java Are We Talking About? There's one more twist. We've spent this article discussing Java, the programming language. But Java is also commonly used to describe an entire development platform. And that platform has a rather complicated family tree. For starters, there's the JVM: the Java Virtual Machine. The thing that executes Java bytecode. Except there isn't just one implementation of the JVM. There's HotSpot, the familiar JVM used by most OpenJDK distributions. There's Eclipse OpenJ9, a separate JVM implementation with different approaches to memory management, startup performance, and garbage collection. Then there's GraalVM, which builds on HotSpot while offering advanced compilation technology and Native Image, a way to compile Java applications into native executables that don't require a JVM at runtime. And that's before we get into the different JDK distributions. Oracle JDK. Eclipse Temurin. Amazon Corretto. Azul Zulu. Microsoft's Build of OpenJDK. Those are distributions rather than entirely separate JVM implementations, but their packaging, support, licensing, and release policies can differ. And then there's the small matter of the languages. Because the Java Virtual Machine doesn't actually require you to write Java. You can use Kotlin. Scala. Clojure. And plenty of others. So now we have one language, multiple JVM implementations, multiple JDK distributions, multiple languages targeting the JVM, and an alternative compilation model that can eliminate the need for a running JVM altogether. Remember Java's original promise? Write once, run anywhere. Turns out the more interesting question is: Run where, on which JVM, from which vendor, using which language? And that's a rabbit hole worthy of its own article. Coming next: the Java ecosystem, where the language is only the beginning of the confusion."}, :excerpts [{:text "Java: When Life Gives You Problems, Build a...", :matched-terms ["java"], :source :title} {:text "... already explored PHP's questionable decisions, JavaScript's complicated relationship with logic,...", :matched-terms ["java"], :source :content} {:text "... and C#'s enterprise-grade surprises. Now it's Java's turn. Java is different. Java is serious. Java is strongly typed, object-oriented, carefully specified, and...", :matched-terms ["java" "typed"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "editor-wars", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://metaredux.com/posts/2026/11/06/clojurists-together-update-september-october-2026", :title "Clojurists Together Update: September and October 2026", :link "https://metaredux.com/posts/2026/11/06/clojurists-together-update-september-october-2026.html", :published-at #inst "2026-11-06T09:00:00.000-00:00", :content "Time for another bi-monthly update on the work that Clojurists Together are funding this year - my maintenance of nREPL, CIDER and friends. You can find the previous one here. For a change, most of my focus in September and October was on nREPL itself, not on CIDER. The big themes were the nREPL protocol spec and how well the servers and clients out there actually stick to it. For years building an nREPL server or client that plays well with the rest of the ecosystem came down to trial and error, and this time I finally did something about it. It paid off much sooner than I expected. The big highlights from my perspective: proof, a compatibility suite for nREPL servers and clients, is out, and it’s already found (and helped fix) problems in nREPL itself, Babashka, CIDER, Calva, Conjure and many other servers and clients neorepl, a brand new terminal REPL meant to be something like “CIDER in a terminal” and “CIDER for agents”, went from 0.1 to 0.4 in two weeks nREPL 1.8 is out, and master already has fixes for some of the bugs proof found in it Piggieback 0.7.1 and 0.8.0 fixed a few long-standing problems with ClojureScript REPLs nrepl.org became a proper landing page (with an nREPL server running right in your browser), the manual moved to docs.nrepl.org, and CIDER got a landing page of its own at cider.mx CIDER’s master gained streaming test results and commands for editing the ns form, backed by new cider-nrepl and Orchard releases Below you’ll find the details, project by project. proof and the nREPL spec proof is the big new project this time around, and the one I’m most excited about. I’ve been talking about some automated way to check nREPL compatibility for ages (it made my list of things people hate about nREPL back in 2023), so it feels really great to finally see it become a reality. Good things take a while to happen, they said… Does your server work with CIDER? Until now the only way to find out was to implement the protocol, connect CIDER (or Calva, or whatever your users run) and see what breaks. Now you can point proof at your server and it’ll tell you. It’s a single binary that runs about 80 checks against a server. A check fails only when a real client would break (with a link to the client code that depends on the behavior), and everything else is just a warning. proof also comes with client profiles that send exactly what CIDER, Calva, Conjure, vim-fireplace, REPLy and neorepl send, profiles for the servers I know of, and a GitHub Action for running it in a server’s CI. Once the server side was in place, I started on the clients as well. proof proxy sits between a client and a server and checks what the client sends, and proof serve is a small server for the test suites of clients, which can mimic the quirks of nREPL, Babashka, jank and others (proof serve -like jank). I wrote about proof in a lot more detail in proof: How Strong Is Your nREPL?. proof 0.1.0 came out on October 26. proof 0.2.0 followed a week later, along with a compatibility matrix that runs proof against every server it knows about daily. proof goes hand in hand with the nREPL spec, the draft Phil Hagelberg wrote earlier this year. When the spec and the existing clients disagree, proof goes with the clients (a spec that breaks CIDER isn’t much use to anyone), and every such conflict, along with the gaps proof ran into, ends up on a list of spec changes. The first few of them are already PRs against the spec: how replies relate to their requests, the status values, how interrupts are answered and the fields of lookup replies. What I like the most about proof is how quickly it started paying off: nREPL itself sent the done of an eval interrupted from another connection to the wrong connection (fixed on master), drops the evals waiting behind an interrupted one (#503) and can’t interrupt an eval that hasn’t started yet (#504). Not great! Between proof and neorepl, Babashka got a couple of dozen issue reports from me in October, from test ops that never answered to a session that stopped answering after its client went away in the middle of an eval. Michiel Borkent fixed nearly all of them the same day or the next. You rock, Michiel! CIDER didn’t finish setting up connections to servers without cider-nrepl (fixed), Calva and Conjure didn’t sort the keys of the dictionaries they sent (both fixed within days), vim-fireplace and REPLy never close their sessions, and REPLy’s and Conjure’s interrupts don’t say which eval to stop. Basilisp and jank got a few reports as well (lookup replies, and a request that throws taking its whole connection down). My own toy implementations (nrepl-beam, mezcaml and neat) were the first projects I ran proof against (more on them below). I was also super happy to see other people using proof already. Tom Waddington has been running it against his nREPL implementations for Janet and Steel Scheme since early October (nrepl-janet even tests its client with proof serve), Jeaye Wilkerson suggested using it for jank’s nREPL server, and Kyle Cesare is already fixing what it found there. That’s exactly what I was hoping for! neorepl neorepl is the other brand new project, and it grew up together with proof. It’s a terminal REPL for Clojure (and anything else that runs an nREPL server), and the best way I can describe it is “CIDER in a terminal”: it uses cider-nrepl when it’s around, the same way CIDER does, and falls back to nREPL’s built-in ops when it isn’t. Every lookup the REPL can do is also a command with structured output, and there’s an MCP server and a skill for coding agents, so it’s “CIDER for agents” as well. Five releases in three weeks: neorepl 0.1.0 had the REPL with a proper line editor (highlighting, completion, arglists as you type, history per project, a Ctrl-C that actually interrupts), CIDER-style REPL commands, jack-in for Leiningen, the Clojure CLI, Babashka and shadow-cljs, and commands for evaluating code and looking things up that keep their session between calls. It runs on the JVM and on Babashka. neorepl 0.2.0 added a test runner (Clojure and ClojureScript), a full-screen inspector and a stacktrace browser. neorepl 0.3.0 was all about coding agents: neorepl mcp, a skill that teaches agents to check, load, test and debug Clojure code with the CLI, and neorepl check --fix, which fixes mismatched delimiters the way parinfer does. neorepl 0.4.0 brought tracing, switching the REPL into another named session (e.g. to take a look at what your coding agent is doing), and a huge list of fixes. I wrote about it (and the two releases before it) in neorepl 0.4: Tests, Inspector, Tracing and Agents. neorepl 0.4.1 caught up with cider-nrepl 0.64. proof checks every server with the exact requests neorepl sends, and neorepl went through proof proxy and every scenario of proof serve before 0.1 was out. Its commands are separate processes sharing a session, so neorepl hits nREPL’s interrupt bugs harder than most clients, and making it work as well on Babashka’s server as with cider-nrepl is where most of the Babashka reports came from. Even JLine got a few bug reports along the way. By the time I got around to writing this update, neorepl 0.5 was out as well, with a much smarter line editor, a namespace browser, automatic reloading and a CLI that editors can use without a plugin. I wrote about it in neorepl 0.5: CIDER in a Terminal. nREPL nREPL 1.8.0 came out on October 2 with the URL-based connections and the TLS improvements I mentioned last time, plus a few session fixes. Sessions survive evaluated code closing *in* or *out*, a race that could split responses between concurrent reads in nrepl.core/client is gone, and Sashko made persistent sessions re-create their thread when a faulty exec thunk kills it. He also moved the Unix sockets to the SocketChannel API. Master moved on quickly after the release, much of it because of proof and neorepl: the done of an eval interrupted from another connection goes to the eval’s own connection, where the client that sent the eval never got it the server sets TCP_NODELAY on accepted connections, so on Linux an eval’s done no longer waits up to 40ms behind its value Sashko replaced :out-limit with separate :out-quota and :err-quota limits, made the bencode transport turn empty lists into nil for ?-ending keys and made building the middleware stack faster The interrupt bugs that are still open are next on my list. Piggieback Piggieback 0.7.1 fixed load-file failing with “No such namespace” when the file requires a foreign lib (e.g. a cljsjs package). Piggieback 0.8.0 fixed :cljs/quit on nREPL 1.3+, where the session stayed in ClojureScript mode, and made evaluation use the same dynamic bindings as cljs.repl. That means the cljs-repl options (e.g. :warnings) and the bindings tools like figwheel-main set up around it finally take effect, which closes an issue from 2018. It also picked up :as-alias keywords and the :cljs branch of data_readers.cljc. nrepl.org and cider.mx nrepl.org got a facelift at the end of October. The manual moved to docs.nrepl.org, and nrepl.org itself is now a landing page that explains what nREPL is and how its protocol works, with real exchanges between a client and a server next to every feature. It even has a “Try it” section with an nREPL server running right in your browser, which turned out to be a story of its own. That playground is how nrepl-sci, a small nREPL server for SCI that runs entirely in a web page, came to be, and it even led me to take a closer look at clojure-mode for CodeMirror, which powers the playground’s editor. CIDER got a landing page of its own at cider.mx, made to look like an Emacs frame (mode line, M-x and all), with mini screencasts of CIDER in action. I had a lot of fun working on both sites! neat, mezcaml and nREPL on the BEAM My toy nREPL implementations were the first things I ran proof against, and all of them got a round of fixes in early October: dialtone and repartee, the Erlang and Elixir servers in nrepl-beam, now answer close with session-closed, echo the op in unknown-op replies and return a value for every Erlang form in an eval mezcaml no longer lets late output from one eval end the next one early, interrupts on Ctrl-C, answers stdin requests, closes its session on exit and completes on jank and Basilisp neat shows late and id-less output, reports error statuses that come without err, handles unknown sessions and stdin EOF, and checks describe before using completions, lookup and interrupt neat’s own integration suite now tests just the client and leaves the servers to proof. None of the three has had a release yet, though. CIDER No CIDER release this time (2.1 is still brewing), but master kept moving: Test results now stream in while the tests run (cider-test-stream-results, on by default): the echo area keeps a running count, and failing tests land in the report as soon as they finish. This closes an issue from 2014! cider-test-fail-fast is now off by default to go with it. A family of commands for editing the ns form: cider-ns-add-require, cider-ns-add-import, cider-ns-add-missing (adds what the symbol at point needs), magic requires (typing alias/ adds the require the alias needs) and cider-ns-clean. These are some of clj-refactor’s most popular features, and they’re backed by new cider-nrepl ops that read your code instead of loading it. Connecting to a server without cider-nrepl (e.g. plain nREPL or Babashka) finishes setting up the connection again, where cider-connected-hook and friends never ran. proof found this one. Keys with a nil value are left out of nREPL requests instead of being sent as empty lists, which closes an issue from 2020. Natively compiled builds no longer have broken menu options, Emacs 31 is on CI, and the manual got per-build-tool setup tabs, the refcard and CI checks for its cross references. cider-nrepl and Orchard cider-nrepl releases: cider-nrepl 0.63.0 runs tests with orchard.test and streams their progress to the client, which is what CIDER’s test streaming builds on. cider-nrepl 0.63.1 was Sashko’s quick bump to compliment 0.8.2. cider-nrepl 0.64.0 added the cider/resolve-missing, cider/resolve-alias and cider/clean-ns ops behind CIDER’s ns commands (after three alphas), made the test ops always answer and send what tests print with their own responses on nREPL 1.3+, and fixed the inspector and ClojureScript tests on shadow-cljs. Orchard releases: Orchard 0.45.0 added orchard.test, the clojure.test runner that used to live in cider-nrepl and can now report progress as it goes, plus Sashko’s string analytics in the inspector and his fix for the stack overflows when printing recursive collections. Orchard 0.46.0 and Orchard 0.47.0 added orchard.ns-form, which takes apart an ns form and tells what it requires, refers and imports that the code doesn’t use, all without loading anything, and fixed some Java doc rendering problems. Orchard 0.48.0 made tracing show calls that throw and stopped tracing a function tracing itself uses (say, str) from overflowing the stack. Sashko also added JDK 27 to the CI of nREPL, cider-nrepl and Orchard. Thanks, Sashko! refactor-nrepl and clj-refactor refactor-nrepl 3.15.0 documented the limitations of its AST-based analysis and deprecated the ops cider-nrepl has taken over. Back in the spring I said I’d like to move the most useful refactor-nrepl features to CIDER and cider-nrepl eventually, and the ns editing commands above are the first step in that direction. clojure-ts-mode No release, but quite a few fixes on main. I fixed clojure-ts-mode on Emacs 31 (e.g. the ClojureScript mode asking for the jsdoc grammar, M-q indenting only the innermost form) and a pile of refactoring commands that went wrong around metadata, #_ forms and reader conditionals. Jason Jackson sent a round of indentation and alignment fixes (threading macros, reader prefixes, metadata and comments before arguments), and Kyle Cesare kept up with jank’s syntax. Thanks, Jason and Kyle! MrAnderson and smaller things MrAnderson 0.7.2 stopped bundling pomegranate’s Maven classes in its jar, where they clashed with any other resolver on the classpath and broke cider-nrepl’s build once tools.build pulled in a newer tools.deps. The artifact went from 8 MB to about 200 KB in the process. There was also a lot of release and CI maintenance across nREPL, cider-nrepl, Orchard, Piggieback and friends (e.g. release notes taken from the changelogs, the docs of a release published by its release workflow), and I revived the CI of parseclj and parseedn. Blog posts neorepl: A Terminal REPL for Clojure (and Friends) neorepl 0.4: Tests, Inspector, Tracing and Agents nREPL, Right in Your Browser neorepl 0.5: CIDER in a Terminal proof: How Strong Is Your nREPL? Epilogue Big thanks to Clojurists Together, Nubank and the other organizations and people supporting my Clojure OSS work! None of this would have happened without you. You rock! As for what’s next - last time I said CIDER 2.1 was the obvious next milestone. It still is, but it got delayed, as most of my time went into nREPL instead. I told you I’m bad at predictions! nREPL 1.8 shipped as planned, and mezcaml and the BEAM servers got a lot more reliable thanks to proof, even if neither got a release. While we’ve made solid progress on CIDER and friends as well, my main focus until the end of the year will likely be the broader nREPL ecosystem: fixing the interrupt bugs proof found in nREPL, turning the list of spec changes into PRs against the spec, and getting more servers and clients to run proof. Keep hacking!"}, :excerpts [{:text "... that send exactly what CIDER, Calva, Conjure, vim-fireplace, REPLy and neorepl send, profiles for...", :matched-terms ["vim"], :source :content} {:text "... dictionaries they sent (both fixed within days), vim-fireplace and REPLy never close their sessions,...", :matched-terms ["vim"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-11-06T18:40:11.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-11-06T18:40:11.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]}]